+## Explicit weight-only INT4 / NF4 DPU inference
+
+The symbolic layer now serializes `i4/u4/nf4` types and provides
+`constant_packed(bytes, shape=..., dtype=...)`: two codes per byte, low nibble
+first, exact ceil(elements/2) bytes, and zero unused high bits for odd counts.
+This does not add integer device allocations or lowbit Program.run IO.
+
+Workspace `torch_npu_lowbit.QuantizedLinear.from_float(cpu_linear, format='nf4')`
+prepares immutable packed INT4/NF4 weights and FP16 scales on CPU. Runtime
+requires 2D intel_npu FP16 activations and returns FP16. A direct driver IR
+graph converts compressed constants, applies scales, reshapes and executes
+MatMul/bias on DPU. Compilation requires the validated 8086:643e / compiler8.3
+profile and nonempty DPU sections. It is independent of the true FP32 carrier
+contract and adds no OpenVINO runtime dependency. Warm calls reuse the weight/
+shape graph and do not reserialize/hash full weights.
+
+Per-output-channel scaling is the default; group sizes must divide K.
+The 8.3 grouped signed INT4 small-M route has reproduced large numerical
+errors, also with one or two tiles. The wrapper conservatively pads grouped
+INT4 M<32 to 32 with device DMA, then trims the result; padding, MatMul and trim
+are now compiled into a single graph submission. Extra storage
+must still fit the allocation bound. This is a tested workaround, not a fix
+to the compiler itself. NF4 needs no such padding in the checked cases.
+
+Twenty numerical checks, two quantized ReLU MLPs, activation/autograd guards,
+eight interleaved timing configurations and 33 original device regressions
+pass. Offline validation has 49 npunlock checks plus three codec/IR checks.
+Large DPU results can differ from a separately rounded FP16-weight graph;
+independent FP64 scaled-code references show <0.03% relative L2 error in the
+measured cases, with an additional weight-rounding error check. This does not
+extend the exact FP32/subnormal guarantee to DPU execution.
+
+Per-channel 1024x1024 graphs have 540672 constant bytes versus 1872896–2031616
+for matched decoded FP16 graphs. Group-128 graphs can expand to approximately
+the same constant footprint as FP16. Lowbit storage alone does not certify
+lower runtime memory or faster execution. See `research/results/lowbit/REPORT.md`.
+The optional `compact_groups=True` mode emits one compressed MatMul per group
+and a balanced FP16 addition tree, with at most 32 groups. It avoids the
+multi-axis grouped lowering path; tested 1024x1024 group128 constants occupy
+655360 bytes instead of approximately 1.9–2.0 MiB. It is disabled by default:
+FP16 partial rounding changes results, and overflowing partials have reproduced
+DPU clipping with incorrect cancellation even when the full dot product is
+finite. Validate partial ranges and model quality before using this experiment.
+Current checks include 42 old/default/compact Linear comparisons, two compact
+MLPs, six exact grouped code tests, two zero tests and two documented clipping
+counterexamples. See `research/results/lowbit-group/REPORT.md` for same-process
+timings, bounds checks and source provenance. No runtime dependencies were added.
+The module supports frozen inference only: no lowbit autograd, QLoRA, ordinary
+state_dict weight persistence, checkpoint-layout import or general lowbit ATen.
+
+## INT8 weights and static W8A8 DPU inference
+
+The workspace wrapper also accepts `format='int8'`: signed bytes with FP16
+scales and the same immutable, frozen-weight interface. W8A16 keeps FP16 IO;
+per-channel compiled matrix tasks on this machine use `amode=0, wmode=2`.
+Passing `activation_scale=1/128` opts into W8A8. The driver lowers input/weight
+FakeQuantize plus MatMul into 8-bit matrix tasks (`amode=wmode=1`, U8 with zero
+point 128). The wrapper inspects every non-elementwise DPU invariant and
+rejects W8A8 graphs whose operands are not 8-bit. It does not claim integer
+Torch storage, bit-exact int32 accumulator output, or lowbit autograd.
+
+W8A8 requires per-channel weights, finite activations and a static power-of-two
+activation step in [2^-24,256]. Values outside [-128*step,127*step] saturate.
+Grouped/compact W8A8 is rejected. Driver 8.3 rounding drift for arbitrary
+activation steps is retained in the diagnostic failures; these steps are
+rejected rather than silently rounded. FP16 weight range endpoints also slightly
+change effective scales. Independent INT64 dot products use aligned integer
+zero points and these effective scales; all-code/tie/saturation checks use exact
+power-of-two scales. The true FP32 contract is unchanged.
+
+25 INT8 numerical checks, three activation-step boundaries, two MLPs, input/cache
+guards, eight large-matrix numerical gates, four interleaved timing cases and
+the original 20 INT4/NF4 plus two MLP regressions passed. Warm contiguous calls
+submit one graph; transposed inputs additionally materialize with device DMA.
+1024x1024 per-channel INT8 graph constants are 1064960 bytes versus 2113536 for
+decoded FP16. Batch512 W8A8 measured 0.824 ms vs NPU FP16 0.962 ms and eight-thread
+CPU FP16 2.135 ms, excluding initial compilation and transfers. This is one
+uncontrolled desktop run, not an LLM or energy benchmark. No runtime dependencies
+were added. See `research/results/int8/REPORT.md`.
+
+Descriptor decoding is tied to the validated compiler8.3 / 8086:643e profile:
+Intel's [NPU40XX hardware enum](https://github.com/openvinotoolkit/npu_compiler/blob/0b38f7d42113ff329ac2bdd33583d123de4ccf2f/src/vpux_compiler/include/vpux/compiler/NPU40XX/dialect/NPUReg40XX/firmware_headers/details/api/vpu_nce_hw_40xx.h)
+and [register offsets](https://github.com/openvinotoolkit/npu_compiler/blob/0b38f7d42113ff329ac2bdd33583d123de4ccf2f/src/vpux_compiler/tblgen/vpux/compiler/NPU40XX/dialect/NPUReg40XX/types.td).
+
+## Explicit frozen causal Transformer inference
+
+Workspace `torch_npu_transformer.FrozenTransformer.from_float(cpu_encoder)`
+snapshots 1-2 pre-norm GELU blocks, D128/256, 32-wide heads, FF=4D, dropout=0,
+batch_first=True, eval mode, with bias and no final Encoder norm. FP16 NPU IO
+is (B,S,D), S<=64; all intermediates must fit the existing 1,048,576-element
+bound. The mask is always causal; no padding mask or KV cache is accepted.
+Each static shape compiles and is retained in the existing bounded graph cache.
+
+Default W8A16 reuses the INT8 weight graph and compiles the entire model to a
+single submission; `mode='fp16'` supplies the baseline. `mode='mixed_int8'`
+requires CPU calibration samples, selects power-of-two activation scales for
+QKV/FFN first projections, keeps the other projections W8A16 and uses existing
+true-FP32 SHAVE normalization at the two boundaries. This mixed route is much
+slower because it submits many graphs; full W8A8 Transformer mode is rejected.
+It adds no OpenVINO runtime dependency and never falls back to CPU arithmetic.
+CPU reference/calibration routines are explicitly separate preparation tools.
+
+Blob inspection proves the presence of INT8 matrix weight operands, and in
+mixed mode both W8A8 and W8A16 matrix operands. It does not map every IR node
+to a DPU invariant: non-elementwise FP16 attention/layout tasks also occur.
+An identical model reusing the global cache still exposes its blob evidence.
+
+15 model/shape/mode numerical checks, fixed-input segments, causality and guards
+passed. Mixed trajectories use a separate 0.5% L2 gate; their fixed-input
+segments retain <0.1% and per-element gates. Earlier stricter whole-model
+failures are retained, not counted as identical-gate passes. On this desktop,
+two D256 blocks, B8/S64, W8A16 measured 1.296 ms vs eight-thread CPU FP32 5.887 ms,
+excluding compilation and transfers. Tiny shapes remain faster on CPU. These
+are random frozen models, not task accuracy, real LLM decoding or power tests.
+See workspace `research/results/transformer/REPORT.md` for details and commands.
+
+This adapter does not implement general softmax/LayerNorm/batched MatMul ATen,
+ordinary Transformer `.to('intel_npu')`, Transformer training, checkpoint layouts,
+INT4/NF4 Transformer or state_dict storage of frozen snapshots. Save the CPU
+source and reconstruct with from_float. The original FP32 device contract and
+lowbit Linear implementation are unchanged.
+
+ABI references: Intel's [SHAVE runtime header](https://github.com/openvinotoolkit/npu_compiler/blob/0b38f7d42113ff329ac2bdd33583d123de4ccf2f/src/vpux_compiler/include/vpux/compiler/NPU40XX/dialect/NPUReg40XX/firmware_headers/details/api/vpu_nnrt_shavert.h)
+and [ELF extensions](https://github.com/openvinotoolkit/npu_compiler_elf/blob/d325f45f2cb405b5fa2ff17a30de9469f1641b73/core/include/vpux_elf/types/vpu_extensions.hpp).
