+## Real SmolLM2-135M checkpoint layer
+
+Workspace `torch_npu_smollm.FrozenSmolLayer.from_checkpoint` loads a frozen
+layer from the pinned original SmolLM2-135M-Instruct safetensors checkpoint.
+It reuses the existing INT8 weight graph, frozen-graph cache/session executor
+and DPU invariant inspection. FP16 and W8A16 are available, with FP16
+(B,S,576) IO, B<=8 and S<=64. The layer implements zero-based RoPE, 9/3-head
+GQA, causal attention, SwiGLU, residuals and genuine FP32 SHAVE RMSNorm with
+the official FP16 rounding/affine order. No CPU arithmetic fallback occurs
+in the adapter forward. CPU loading, quantization and RoPE constants are
+explicit preparation. No OpenVINO runtime is installed or imported.
+
+This is explicit frozen-layer support, not a general Llama ATen backend.
+Arbitrary positions/masks, padding, training and whole-model NPU generation
+are not supported. Bounded KV caching uses the explicit API described below.
+The original demo's hybrid generation runs one NPU
+layer plus 29 CPU layers and CPU embedding/final norm/logits, with explicit
+transfers. Two prompts in each precision reproduced the CPU generation;
+this is a smoke check, not broad language-model evaluation.
+
+Numerical gates distinguish execution from quantization and retain the
+original fixed-atol failures. The final whole-layer gate is L2<0.3% and
+abs(error)/(abs(reference)+per-token RMS)<1% for every element; fixed-input
+segments use L2<0.1%, and quantization quality against the official CPU
+floating layer uses L2<3%. This does not imply bit-exact FP16 equivalence.
+The true FP32 device contract and existing lowbit Linear are unchanged.
+
+The first unfused timing/profile is retained in workspace
+`npunlock-probe/research/results/smollm/REPORT.md`. The adapter now defaults
+to a fused SHAVE RMSNorm with FP16 IO and genuine FP32 compensated mean,
+sqrt/reciprocal and normalization. The initial fusion used normal hardware
+half conversion; subnormals/zero/overflow retain bit-preserving helpers. The intermediate
+normalized value is rounded to half before the half gamma product.
+
+On 2026-10-05, `compile_rmsnorm(rows,kernel_elf)` defaults to the compiler's
+unmodified DefaultHW Power schedule for 8..512 rows: four tiles, two complete
+row SHAVE tasks per tile. Inputs smaller than eight rows retain ReferenceSW,
+because DefaultHW splits a single row's width. The optional `parallel=False`
+retains the <=256-row ReferenceSW carrier for numerical/performance comparison.
+Gamma is pre-broadcast. Each task must have three complete contiguous
+576-wide memrefs; per-tile output spans must not overlap and shard row counts
+must sum to the input rows. No DPU is allowed. The existing Power fingerprint,
+CMX/layout/profile checks and code-window-only replacement remain in force.
+There are no edits to DMA, barriers or scheduling. Generic operation/storage
+limits are unchanged. Warm whole-layer calls now submit four graphs even at
+B8/S64; no extra device slicing/assembly is needed in the default path.
+
+Twenty-two norm checks cover the 7/8/9-row scheduling boundary, uneven row
+counts, maximum dimensions, half subnormals, zeros and large squares; they
+matched the old NPU output bits. Real-layer gates, maximum dimensions,
+causality, guards and hybrid generation use the unchanged tolerances.
+The benchmark compares distributed and serial fusion, old unfused W8A16,
+and CPU references in the same process. That scheduling stage kept the kernel C/ELF unchanged.
+
+An isolated four-lane reduction also emits VAU.f32 instructions. Its first
+half-vector conversion lowered to u16->f32 and failed the numerical gate.
+Using validated scalar conversion fixed the values, but yielded no stable
+latency benefit; it remains a research prototype. That stage kept
+the scalar kernel and distributed complete rows. Evidence,
+timings and commands are in workspace
+`npunlock-probe/research/results/rmsnorm-vector/REPORT.md`; the earlier serial
+fusion stage remains in `npunlock-probe/research/results/rmsnorm/REPORT.md`.
+
+The subsequent output-loop optimization is now the default kernel. Four
+elements are decoded/rounded together using integer vector bit operations
+for the normal range, avoiding the faulty half-vector conversion builtin.
+FP32 output products use VAU; exceptional conversions retain the original
+scalar helpers. Compensated reduction order, FP32 sqrt/reciprocal, the half
+rounding before gamma, and complete-row scheduling are unchanged.
+All 63,488 finite half encodings have roundtrip checks; old/new norm and
+whole-layer output bits are compared. In one same-process confirmation,
+256/512-row real-embedding norm speedups were 1.59/1.64x with reused output;
+the B4/S64 W8A16 layer improved from 9.620 to 6.994 ms. These are warm
